<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="calcwparammq" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Mini-review of course topics: [PATHS]  Calculus with parametrized curves.</title>

<p>(This is one of the two options that earn the <q>WILD any calculus</q> sticker in the <q>PATHS</q> category.)  <em>The overall idea:  Throughout our Calculus I and Calculus II experience, we have developed ways to discover various geometric aspects of graphs that are expressed in the form <q><m>y</m> as a function of <m>x</m>.</q>  We can discover <em>exactly</em> the same geometric aspects of graphs that are expressed via parametric equations; we just have to adapt our <q><m>xy</m>-notions</q> appropriately.</em></p>

Memory work<mdash/>do this <em>NOW</em>, <em>OUT LOUD</em>:
<ul>
<li>  <em>EXPLAIN</em> the tricks of <q>using <m>\frac{dx}{dt} \cdot dt</m> whenever you need <m>\Delta x</m></q> and <q>using <m>\frac{dy}{dt} \cdot dt</m> whenever you need <m>\Delta y</m>.</q>
<li>  <em>STATE</em> the six formulas given in the supplement.
</ul>
<p>For the quiz, be ready to do the following:
<ul>
<li>  Given parametric equations for a curve and a basic sketch of the curve, set up integrals representing the following six quantities <em>in terms of <m>t</m>:
<ul>
<li>  <m>\displaystyle{\frac{dy}{dx}}</m> (the slope of the curve's tangent lines); </li>
<li>  the length of the curve; </li>
<li>  the volume of the solid generated by revolving the region under the curve around the <m>x</m>-axis; </li>
<li>  <m>\displaystyle{\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}}</m> (the concavity of the curve); </li>
<li>  the area under the curve; and </li>
<li>  the surface area of the surface generated by revolving the curve around the <m>x</m>-axis.  (This last one will not be <em>REQUIRED</em> until we have seen <q>general</q> surface area in class, but it will always be an <em>OPTION</em>.)  </li>
</ul>
You will not have to find <em>ALL</em> of them on any given quiz; generally you will be asked for <em>TWO</em>.  But be ready to give <em>ANY</em> of them.</p>
</ul>
<p>Other consideration:
<ul>
<li>    <em>More</em> helpful than simply memorizing the six formulas:  Practice <em>deriving</em> all of them (many with the <q>green common thread</q> in mind)!
</ul></p>

<subsection><title>Examples</title>

<example><p>Do BOTH of the following.
<ul>
<li>  Give a parametrization of an ellipse with horizontal axis 14 units long and vertical axis 10 units long, centered at the point <m>(1,4)</m>, letting <m>t</m> vary from <m>0</m> to <m>2\pi</m>.</li>
<li>  Eliminate the parameter to obtain an equation in <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>:
<me> x = 1 - \sin^2t,  y = \cos t,  \pi \leq t \leq 2\pi. </me>
Then <em>graph</em> the curve.</li>
</ul></p>
</example>

<example><p>Do BOTH of the following.
<ul>
<li>  Give a parametrization of the circle of radius <m>6</m> with center <m>(3,-1)</m>, letting <m>t</m> vary from <m>0</m> to <m>2\pi</m>, starting at the <em>left</em> side of the circle (the point <m>(-3,-1)</m>) and using <m>clockwise</m> orientation. </li>
<li>  Give a parametrization of the line segment from the point <m>(9,4)</m> to the point <m>(6,7)</m>.  Be sure to give a domain for <m>t</m>-values. </li>
</ul></p>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>